"In this section, the verses addressing the believers that begin with Allah Almighty's command, \"O you who believe!\" are examined. In his tafsir, al-Razi notes that there is a beautiful connection in the arrangement of these verses: An obstinate person either persists in their obstinacy, abandons it, or shows submission. These verses inform the believers how to treat them according to each situation: (60/4) points to the first, (60/7) to the second, and (60/10) to the third situation. Then, it is commanded to examine the believing women who arrive having migrated. This examination is intended to reveal the sincerity of their faith. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would have them swear an oath in this manner: \"Do you swear that you did not set out out of hatred for a husband, nor desiring one place over another, nor for any purpose other than love for Allah and His Messenger?\"\n\nThe reason for this examination is that the primary condition of faith is the affirmation of the heart, and only Allah knows the secrets in the heart. The believers conduct this test to attain outward knowledge. If, as a result of the examination, the women are found to be believers, they must not be returned to the disbelievers. For believing women are not lawful for disbelievers, nor are disbelievers lawful for believing women. This verse specifically protects women from this prohibition and commands the compensation of the expenses (mahr) incurred by the disbelieving husbands. However, this compensation applies to parties that have concluded a treaty, such as the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah. Indeed, throughout the treaty's duration, the Messenger of Allah returned the men who came from Quraysh, while regarding the women, this verse was revealed.\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, it is stated that there is no sin upon you in marrying believing women, provided you give them their dowers (mahrs). As for disbelieving women, do not hold fast to marital bonds with them ('ismah); that is, do not maintain the bond of marriage with them. Furthermore, it is commanded to mutually claim and exchange the dowers spent on the migrating believing women. According to the majority of exegetes, the expression \"if you have your turn (mu'aqib)\" means \"if the turn comes to you.\" That is, if your women flee to the disbelievers and then their women migrate to you, the turn to compensate falls upon you, and you pay this compensation from the spoils of war or similar means. Fear Allah and abide by His judgment.\n\nNext, the verse regarding the pledge of allegiance (bay'ah) of the believing women (60/12) is explained. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) accepted the pledge from women upon the following conditions: not to associate anything with Allah, not to steal, not to commit adultery, not to kill their children, not to bring forth a slander they have fabricated between their hands and feet, and not to disobey the Messenger of Allah in any good deed. During this pledge, the Prophet (peace be upon him) did not shake hands (musafahah) with women; he used a piece of cloth or water. What transpired during the pledge of Hind bint 'Utbah is recounted: Hind raised certain objections, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) smiled and pardoned her.\n\nIn the final section of the surah, the verse, \"O you who believe! Do not take as allies a people with whom Allah is angry\" is addressed. This phrase describes the disbelievers who have despaired of the Hereafter. The phrase, \"just as the disbelievers have despaired of those in the graves\" has been interpreted in two ways: First, that the disbelievers who enter the grave completely lose hope upon seeing their place in Hellfire; second, that the disbelievers have despaired of the resurrection of the dead. Despair is disbelief (kufr) and invites the wrath of Allah. Following this, Surah as-Saff is introduced: It was revealed in Medina, and is also known as \"al-Hawariyyun\" and \"Surah 'Isa\". It contains fourteen verses, two hundred and twenty words, and nine hundred and twenty-six letters. There are three narrations regarding its occasion of revelation: the Companions asking which deed is most beloved, certain young men speaking of things they had not done, and the actions of the hypocrites contradicting their words.\n\nSurah as-Saff encourages the believers to strive (jihad) in the way of Allah with truthfulness and loyalty. At the end of the previous surah, Surah al-Imtihan, hope in the Hereafter was strengthened by warning against despair. In this surah, in order to prove the supremacy of the religion of Islam before all the worlds, Muslims are commanded to align themselves in ranks like a solid structure and take their place on the fields of jihad. Everything that glorifies Allah proclaims that He is the Almighty, the All-Wise.\n\n\"O you who believe! Why do you say what you do not do?\" There are various narrations concerning this verse: in the narration of 'Abdullah ibn Salam, it is emphasized that vows must be fulfilled; in the narration of al-Dahhak, lying is condemned; and in another narration, the hypocrites are rebuked. The most correct view is that it refers to vows made regarding the future. That is, when a legitimate act is vowed, it becomes obligatory (wajib); once vowed, it must certainly be fulfilled.\n\nAllah loves those who fight in His way, lined up in ranks as though they were a solid structure cemented together. When Moses (Musa) said to his people, \"Why do you hurt me?\", as they deviated, Allah caused their hearts to deviate. Jesus ('Isa) likewise heralded to the Children of Israel a messenger to come after him whose name would be Ahmad. But when clear proofs came to them, they said, \"This is obvious magic.\" There will be those who wish to extinguish the light of Allah, but Allah will complete His light and make His Messenger prevail over all religions.\n\n\"O you who believe! When believing women come to you as emigrants, examine them. Have them take an oath to ascertain that their hearts match their tongues. If you are satisfied of their faith, do not return them to the disbelievers; for believing women are not lawful for disbelievers, nor are disbelievers lawful for believers. Pay them the dower that the disbelievers spent on those women. This ruling pertains to the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah. Allah judges between you, and He is the All-Knowing, the All-Wise.\"\n\n\"O Prophet! When believing women come to you to pledge allegiance, take their pledge upon the conditions that they will not associate anything with Allah, will not steal, will not commit adultery, will not kill their children, will not bring forth a slander they have fabricated between their hands and feet, and will not disobey you in what is right.\" The Prophet (peace be upon him) did not shake hands with women. What occurred during the pledge of Hind bint 'Utbah is an example of the implementation of this verse. Ask forgiveness from Allah for the women who pledge allegiance.\n\n\"O you who believe! Fear Allah and abide by His decree.\" Allah Almighty states in a hadith qudsi: \"O My servants! I have forbidden injustice to Myself, so do not wrong one another. I conceal your sins with My forgiveness; seek forgiveness from Me so that I may forgive you. I preserve your deeds; whoever finds good, let him praise Allah, and whoever finds evil, let him blame no one but himself.\" Indeed, the meaning of this surah is sincerity and being like a solid structure.\n\nThe believers must not take as allies a people who have incurred Allah's wrath. Although this expression is generally used for the Jews, the hypocrites or the polytheists of Quraysh may also be intended here. What is most important is that those who have despaired of the Hereafter can commit every kind of evil; therefore, the believers must beware of such despair.\n\nDespairing of the Hereafter is disbelief (kufr) and incurs the wrath of Allah. Indeed, it is also stated in Surah Yusuf: \"For none despairs of Allah's mercy except the disbelieving people.\" The believers must remain hopeful of Allah's mercy and be filled with the joy of faith.\n\nSurah as-Saff was revealed in Medina and consists of 14 verses. There are three narrations regarding the occasion of the surah's revelation: First, that it was revealed when the Companions asked which deed was most beloved to Allah; second, that certain youths spoke of things they had not done; and third, the hypocritical behavior of the hypocrites. The most authentic narration is the first one, which comes from Abdullah b. Salam.\n\nThe primary objective of the surah is to encourage striving (jihad) in the cause of Allah with truthfulness and devotion, and to illuminate the future of Islam. The believers must fight interlocking together like \"bunyan marsus,\" that is, a solid structure. This metaphor expresses both physical order and unity of heart; behaviors that disrupt love and solidarity among the believers must be avoided.\n\nThe verse, \"Why do you say that which you do not do?\" warns particularly regarding vows. Believers should not vow what they cannot do, and when they do make a vow, they should fulfill it immediately. This is to avoid lying, which is a major sin in the sight of Allah.\n\nAllah loves those who fight in His cause standing in ranks. This rank must be as solid as a wall joined together with lead. The name of the surah, \"as-Saff\" (The Rank), also points to this unity. The mujahideen must unite around the same objective and act in this manner to earn Allah's love.\n\nMoses asked his people why they hurt him even though they knew he was Allah's messenger to them. When they did not turn toward the truth, Allah caused their hearts to deviate. This demonstrates that the transgressors (fasiqun) will not be guided to the right path. Muslims must be prepared to fight against such corrupted communities.\n\nJesus stated that he was sent to the Children of Israel, confirming the Torah, and heralding the glad tidings of a messenger to come after him whose name is Ahmad. The Jews rejected him, and the Christians altered this glad tidings. The name Ahmad means praiseworthy and is among the names of the Prophet Muhammad.\n\nThe word 'Paraclete' (Faraklit) mentioned in the Gospels points to Ahmad or Muhammad. Although Christians translate this as 'the Comforter', in reality, this word derives from the root hamd and heralds the Prophet Muhammad. In the face of this clear declaration of the Quran, it is understood that the Gospels have been corrupted.\n\nJesus (peace be upon him) had announced the glad tidings to the Children of Israel that a messenger named Ahmad would come after him. However, just as the Jews refused to heed this glad tidings, most of the Christians also concealed it or corrupted and denied it. Finally, when that heralded messenger (Muhammad, peace be upon him) came with clear proofs, the Jews and Christians deviated into denial, saying, 'This is obvious magic.' Furthermore, fabricating lies against Allah, they invented falsehoods such as attributing a son to Him and presented these as the word of Allah.\n\nWho can be more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allah or fabricates falsehoods concerning Him? Allah does not guide such wrongdoers to the right path, nor does He grant them success. Indeed, the injustices of these deniers are among the reasons that necessitate the believers' preparing for war in the way of Allah and make striving a beloved deed. They wish to extinguish the light of Allah with their mouths, but Allah will perfect His light, even if the disbelievers dislike it.\n\nAllah has sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth to cause it to prevail over all religions. Addressing the believers, it is proclaimed: Shall I guide you to a transaction that will save you from a painful punishment? That transaction is: to believe in Allah and His Messenger, and to strive in the cause of Allah with your wealth and your lives. That is better for you, if you only knew. Striving may feel difficult, but something you dislike may be good for you.\n\nThrough this faith and striving, Allah forgives your sins and admits you into Gardens beneath which rivers flow and into pleasant dwellings in the Gardens of Eden (Adn). That is the supreme triumph. Moreover, there is another blessing that you will love: help from Allah and an imminent conquest. So give these glad tidings to the believers!\n\nO you who believe! Be supporters of Allah. Just as Jesus, the son of Mary, said to the disciples, 'Who are my supporters for Allah?' and the disciples replied, 'We are the supporters of Allah.' Thereupon, a group from the Children of Israel believed, while another group disbelieved. Allah supported those who believed, and they became victorious. So be like that as well.\n\nThe disciples (Hawariyyun) were the distinguished first disciples of Jesus who believed, numbering twelve individuals. This name was given to them on account of their sincerity and purity. Some scholars have stated that they received this name because of the white garments they wore, or because they cleansed people with religion and knowledge. The disciples were sent to various regions and spread the message of Jesus (peace be upon him). Later figures like Paul, who deviated into false beliefs, are not counted among the disciples.\n\nThe Companions of our Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) became supporters of Allah just like the disciples, and in a short time they overcame the polytheists and the Christians, causing Islam to prevail. This superiority is not confined solely to that era, but encompasses all believers who act in this manner until the Day of Resurrection. The help of Allah is with those who help His religion. In order to prevail over their enemies, believers must rely on this divine promise and seek their shortcomings in their own sins."